.TH "GDAL_SIEVE" "1" "Aug 18, 2026" "" "GDAL"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
gdal_sieve \- Removes small raster polygons.
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
gdal_sieve [\-\-help] [\-\-help\-general]
              [\-q] [\-st threshold] [\-4] [\-8] [\-o name=value]
              <srcfile> [\-nomask] [\-mask filename] [\-of format] [<dstfile>]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
.sp
\fBgdal_sieve\fP removes raster polygons smaller than
a provided threshold size (in pixels) and replaces them with the
pixel value of the largest neighbour polygon. The result can be written
back to the existing raster band, or copied into a new file.
.sp
The input dataset is read as integer data which means that floating point
values are rounded to integers. Re\-scaling source data may be necessary in
some cases (e.g. 32\-bit floating point data with min=0 and max=1).
.sp
Additional details on the algorithm are available in the \fI\%GDALSieveFilter()\fP docs.
.sp
\fBNOTE:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
gdal_sieve is a Python utility, and is only available if GDAL Python bindings are available.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBTIP:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Equivalent in new \(dqgdal\(dq command line interface:
.sp
See \fI\%gdal raster sieve\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH RETURN STATUS CODE
.sp
The program returns status code 0 in case of success, and non\-zero in case of
error (non\-blocking errors emitted as warnings are considered as a successful
execution).
